USB Wi-Fi interface naming on the LEC-MTK-i1200
===============================================

A USB wireless adapter is named after the module USB connection it is
plugged into. The name belongs to the socket rather than to the adapter:
it is the same on every unit and after every boot, and it is known
before the units are built, so it can be written into a shared image.

  Module USB connection   Interface name   Where the adapter attaches
  ---------------------   --------------   --------------------------
  SMARC USB0              wlan-usb0        SoC host controller 112a0000
  SMARC USB1              wlan-usb1        SoC host controller 112b0000
  SMARC USB2              wlan-usb2        module hub, downstream port 3
  SMARC USB3              wlan-usb3        module hub, downstream port 4
  SMARC USB4              wlan-usb4        module hub, downstream port 5
  SMARC USB5              wlan-usb5        module hub, downstream port 6

Everything the naming keys on is fixed on the module, so the table holds
on any carrier board. Which carrier socket each connection reaches is a
property of the carrier. To find the name for a socket, plug an adapter
into it and read the name:

  # ls /sys/class/net/ | grep '^wl'

To see which policy file named an interface, and so which connection it
is on:

  # udevadm info /sys/class/net/wlan-usb3 | grep ID_NET_LINK_FILE

An adapter that is not attached directly to one of the six connections
-- one behind a hub on the carrier board -- is named from its own
hardware address instead, as wlx followed by the twelve hexadecimal
digits of that address. Such a name is unique to the adapter, so it
differs from unit to unit and cannot be written into a shared image.

The policy files are in /usr/lib/systemd/network/. A file of your own in
/etc/systemd/network/ takes precedence only if it carries the same file
name as the one it replaces, or sorts before 60-wlan-usb0.link.

Because the name belongs to the socket, moving an adapter to a different
connection renames it, and so does a carrier revision that moves a
socket to a different connection. A connection profile that should
survive either matches a set of names rather than naming one:

  [match]
  interface-name=wlan-usb*;wlx*

Wired interfaces are not affected by any of this.
